            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Observed Air Leakage: Overall, the air leakage of the house was quite low and might not be too beneficial to reduce it more without also installing an HRV. If there are any drafty areas of the house, feel free to fix the air leakage, itÂs just not going to have much savings and isnÂt necessary to focus much time on it.

A leaky spot that you could look into is the downstairs bathroom fan. It should have a damper or flaps on the outside that might not be closing fully. Removing any ice buildup might solve this problem.

Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $ 60 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within three months of the second energy evaluation. For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2 inches (R10) of extruded polystyrene insulation. I have modelled adding another 2 inches (R10) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.





Savings:

By adding R10 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially save $ 225 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,875 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</mainWalls>
                <foundation>Your crawlspace walls are 2x4 framing with no insulation. I have modelled adding 4 inches (R12) fiberglass batt insulation to the interior of your crawlspace walls.





Savings:

By adding 4 inches (R12) batt insulation to the crawlspace walls, you could potentially save $ 40 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate 100 percent of the crawl space wall area, including the header area. The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <VentilationSystem>I did not see a combustion air supply pipe to provide combustion air to the hot water tank.

If changes are being made to the heating system, you may be required to install a combustion air supply. If this is the case, the combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the boiler or hot water tank is operating.</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>I recommend that you replace your propane hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing propane water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.





Savings:

By installing an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially save $ 265 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 8.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>Propane Boiler



Your current propane furnace was originally rated at 82% AFUE. If you are considering installing a boiler system, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. A boiler would require running pipes to the various areas of the house and installing baseboard or unit heater radiators. This would be a much more expensive project than a one-for-one replacement of the furnace but it would help to distribute the heat throughout the house evenly.





Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 860 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 27.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Pellet Stove



The propane insert on the second floor is used as a supplementary heating system. Another option for this would be to replace it with a wood pellet stove or insert. Wood pellets are a cheap heating fuel and are more convenient than cordwood. 



Savings:

By replacing the propane fireplace with a pellet stove or insert and use it for about 50% of your heating needs, you could potentially save $ 995 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7.8 GJ, and qualify for 1/3rd the cost up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>Fuel Costs - Yellowknife

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $18.70 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Nick Walker

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: nick.walker@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
